Objective:To work with exception handling.Concept Explanation:The finally block in Java always executes, regardless of exceptions or return statements within try or catch blocks. It's commonly used for resource cleanup, post-execution actions, and to avoid code duplication.Concept Implementation:Here, the use of the finally block for exception handling, specifically to gracefully handle potential parsing errors when processing user-input dates with the SimpleDateFormat class in Java. Regardless of the success or failure of date parsing, the finally block ensures that the application always responds to the user.Scenario:Implement the Java code to parse the date from a string to a date using the date formats available in Java and handle the exceptions using exception handling mechanisms.public static String validateDate(String date): This method checks if the provided date string follows the dd/MM/yyyy format. If the format is correct, return "Date Validated". Otherwise, return "Error: Ensure date is in the format dd/MM/yyyy".In the UserInterface class, implement the main method to call validateDate() and display the returned message.Display the message "Thanks for using the application." at the end, regardless of the date format.Notes:In the Sample Input / Output provided, the highlighted text in bold corresponds to the input given by the user, and the rest of the text represents the output. Ensure to follow the object-oriented specifications provided in the question description.Ensure to provide the names for the classes, attributes, and methods as specified in the question descriptionAdhere to the code template, if provided.Do not use System.exit(0).Sample Input / Output 1Enter the date31/12/2000Date Validated. Thanks for using the application.Sample Input / Output 2Enter the date1-1-2020Error: Ensure date is in the format dd/MM/yyyy. Thanks for using the application.
